===Noun===
{{en-noun|solidi|+}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} Various [[medieval]] and early [[modern]] [[coin]]s or [[units of account]], ''particularly:''
## A [[Roman]] ~23[[carat|k]] [[gold coin]] introduced by [[Diocletian]] in <small>[[AD]]</small>&nbsp;301 and called by that name, but reissued at a slightly lower weight by Constantine I.
## Its [[successor]] [[Byzantine]] [[gold coin|coin]]s, from the eleventh century onward of [[progressive]]ly [[debase]]d [[weight]] and [[purity]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{synonym of|en|sol}} or [[sou]]: a [[Carolingian]] unit of account equivalent to a {{l|en|solidus|id=French weight}} of [[silver]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{synonym of|en|soldo}}: the silver coins of various Italian states.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{synonym of|en|shilling}}: an English unit of account and, following the [[Tudor]] [[dynasty]], silver coin.
# {{lb|en|historical}} The weight of the [[Roman]] gold coin, 1/60 of a [[Roman pound]] under [[Diocletian]] or 1/72 [[lb]]. (about 4.5 [[gram]]s) after [[Constantine]].
# {{senseid|en|French weight}} {{lb|en|historical}} A medieval French weight, 1/20 of the [[Carolingian pound]].
# {{lb|en|typography}} {{synonym of|en|slash}} ⟨'''[[/]]'''⟩, originally {{lb|en|UK}} in its use as the [[shilling mark]] and now its formal designation by the [[ISO]] and [[Unicode]].
# {{lb|en|typography}} The formal name of the [[oblique]] [[strikethrough]] [[overlay]] (as in A̷ and B̸) in [[Unicode]].
# {{lb|en|typography}} The [[division]] [[line]] between the [[numerator]] and the [[denominator]] of a [[fraction]], whether [[horizontal]] or [[oblique]].
# {{lb|en|chemistry|physics}} The line in a [[phase diagram]] marking the [[temperature]]s and [[pressure]]s below which a given substance is a [[stable]] [[solid]].
#: {{cot|en|liquidus|solvus}}

